```

That is 16 `+` lines and 1 `-` line. Every other line of the file (the header comment, `settledWithin`, cell 1, the
titles) stays byte-identical.

Why each cell is what it is, after the edit:
- **Cell 2 (🔴)** at the tip: the lookup answers after 200 ms, the request never answers, and the guard's deadline fires
  after the REMAINING ~200 ms, so the call returns `request_failed` / `request exceeded its 400ms deadline …` at about
  400 ms, with exactly one `https.request`. Under the tamper, the deadline gets the FULL 400 ms after DNS, so the call
  takes about 600 ms and `expect(elapsed).toBeLessThan(550)` fails **by assertion**. The `>= 380` floor and the call count
  make a fast failure impossible to pass: nothing can end the call early except the deadline.
- **Cell 3 (control)** is green at the tip AND under the tamper: the lookup answers at once, so the remaining budget and
  the full budget are the same 300 ms. It proves the mock reaches the transport exactly once and that the only way out is
  the deadline.
- **Cell 1 (🔴 KS-932, unchanged)** never reaches the transport (the lookup never answers). It is green under this
  tamper; it is the red-proof for a DIFFERENT tamper (the race removed). That is expected, not a failure.

## Red cells

- 🔴 KS-932 — the budget is shared: a slow lookup leaves less for the request

## Tamper (test-only mode — the checker plants this in a clone to prove your cells can go red)

line: 576
from: `    }, remainingMs);`
to: `    }, timeoutMs);`
statement_ok: the `from` line closes the `deadline = setTimeout(() => {` call opened at `:569`, whose body `:570-575` destroys the request and settles `request_failed` with the `${timeoutMs}ms deadline` text; `to` keeps that callback and only changes the delay from the remainder (`:493`) to the full `timeoutMs`. Nothing else can end the call early under the mocked transport: `done` is otherwise reached only from a response `end` (`:540`), a request `error` event (`:578`) or a synchronous throw (`:544`, `:587`), and the never-answering mock produces none of them; the DNS timer was cleared at `:483` before the request. This is byte-for-byte the gate's T2 (`evidence/s3_tampers_flake.py:63`).

## Output

Exactly ONE ```diff block containing ONLY the modified test file (`--- a/…` / `+++ b/…`, path repo-relative
`Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks932-timeout-bounds-dns.test.ts`); the FOUR hunks exactly as the
headers above say (E3 is one hunk); blank context lines keep their leading space; correct hunk counts. No hunk for
